WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.320 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A designer spins a square logo and notices that it can look
unchanged before completing a full turn.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.160 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Would every rectangle behave the same way?

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:18.470 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we will investigate rotations and rotational symmetry,
using a fixed centre and clear turn directions instead of
judging a shape only at rest.

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:18.950 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to describe turns and test rotational
symmetry.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:30.350 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will move from objects or hands-on actions to drawings,
then connect those drawings with precise mathematical
descriptions.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:31.750 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep paper nearby if you would like to test an idea for
yourself.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:37.310 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can pause the player whenever you need more time.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:41.710 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to describe turns and test rotational
symmetry.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:44.550 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want to explain why a result is correct, as well as
obtain the result.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:51.943 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you watch, look for the evidence that would help you
convince a partner who has not seen your working.

00:00:51.943 --> 00:00:54.623 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Here are our three success criteria.

00:00:54.623 --> 00:00:56.743 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can name the centre and turn.

00:00:56.743 --> 00:00:59.023 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can track corresponding points.

00:00:59.023 --> 00:01:01.303 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can count matching positions.

00:01:01.303 --> 00:01:05.143 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will check these ideas in two worked examples,
investigate a common mistake and finish with an independent
challenge.

00:01:05.143 --> 00:01:13.868 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A good explanation links what you see in the model with the
mathematical rule being used.

00:01:13.868 --> 00:01:18.668 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Trace a simple shape, mark a centre and hold that point
still with a pencil tip.

00:01:18.668 --> 00:01:21.868 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Turn the tracing around the centre without sliding it.

00:01:21.868 --> 00:01:24.388 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every point follows a circular path around the same fixed
position.

00:01:24.388 --> 00:01:30.545 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The original outline remains as a reference for comparing
the new position.

00:01:30.545 --> 00:01:36.185 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A useful rotation instruction names the centre, the size of
the turn and its direction.

00:01:36.185 --> 00:01:44.145 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A quarter turn is ninety degrees, a half turn is one hundred
and eighty degrees, and a full turn is three hundred and
sixty degrees.

00:01:44.145 --> 00:01:47.296 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Clockwise follows the direction of clock hands.

00:01:47.296 --> 00:01:51.496 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
During a rotation, each point keeps its own distance from
the centre.

00:01:51.496 --> 00:01:56.536 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Different points may have different distances, but every
point turns through the same angle.

00:01:56.536 --> 00:02:00.696 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This distinguishes a turn from moving corners independently.

00:02:00.696 --> 00:02:04.421 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Side lengths and angles of the whole shape remain unchanged.

00:02:04.421 --> 00:02:04.701 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The centre is four, four.

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:10.381 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Point A is at six, four, directly two units to its right.

00:02:10.381 --> 00:02:14.421 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict where A lands after a quarter turn anticlockwise.

00:02:14.421 --> 00:02:20.173 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine the radius turning from pointing right to pointing
up, while keeping the same two-unit length.

00:02:25.173 --> 00:02:26.933 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The radius turns upward, so A lands at four, six.

00:02:26.933 --> 00:02:29.973 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It remains two units from the centre.

00:02:29.973 --> 00:02:33.093 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The centre itself stays at four, four.

00:02:33.093 --> 00:02:40.579 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Turning the whole shape means using that same quarter turn
for every corner, rather than treating A as the new centre
for the next point.

00:02:40.579 --> 00:02:44.299 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Starting again from six, four, a clockwise quarter turn
points the radius downward.

00:02:44.299 --> 00:02:47.459 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The image is four, two.

00:02:47.459 --> 00:02:54.019 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both images are the same distance from the centre, but their
positions differ because the turn directions differ.

00:02:54.019 --> 00:02:58.774 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Always include direction when a quarter turn could be
interpreted either way.

00:02:58.774 --> 00:02:59.894 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A student turns the tracing around one corner after being
given the centre of the grid.

00:02:59.894 --> 00:03:09.054 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The new shape may still have the same side lengths, but it
is not the requested rotation.

00:03:09.054 --> 00:03:12.814 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Put the pencil back on the named centre and repeat the turn.

00:03:12.814 --> 00:03:15.560 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Location matters as well as angle.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:22.040 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A shape has a matching position when turning it around its
centre makes it cover its original outline exactly.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:27.480 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We count distinct matches during one full turn, including
the final full-turn match.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.755 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our convention calls a shape with only that final match
order one.

00:03:31.755 --> 00:03:35.435 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine turning an unmarked square around its centre.

00:03:35.435 --> 00:03:39.595 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
At which quarter-turn stages will the outline cover the
original?

00:03:39.595 --> 00:03:45.835 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count the matching positions during one full turn, but do
not count the starting position as an extra match.

00:03:45.835 --> 00:03:50.596 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Prepare to explain each position rather than guess from the
number of sides.

00:03:55.596 --> 00:04:02.156 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The square matches after ninety, one hundred and eighty, two
hundred and seventy, and three hundred and sixty degrees.

00:04:02.156 --> 00:04:05.316 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That gives rotational symmetry of order four.

00:04:05.316 --> 00:04:07.516 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We count the full-turn match once.

00:04:07.516 --> 00:04:13.599 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Counting both zero degrees and the full turn as separate end
positions would incorrectly give five.

00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:20.719 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A non-square rectangle fails to match after a quarter turn
because its long direction has become its short direction.

00:04:20.719 --> 00:04:24.319 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It matches after a half turn and after a full turn.

00:04:24.319 --> 00:04:29.962 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
There are two matching positions in one full turn, so its
rotational symmetry has order two.

00:04:29.962 --> 00:04:31.402 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A reflection uses a mirror line and reverses orientation.

00:04:31.402 --> 00:04:34.122 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rotation uses a centre and a turn direction.

00:04:34.122 --> 00:04:43.522 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A shape may have both kinds of symmetry, but a line of
reflection is not a centre of rotation.

00:04:43.522 --> 00:04:47.304 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Name the transformation before explaining what stays the
same.

00:04:47.304 --> 00:04:51.584 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An ordinary uneven shape may only match after one full turn.

00:04:51.584 --> 00:04:54.064 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Under our convention that is order one.

00:04:54.064 --> 00:04:58.024 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Do not assume every shape has a useful smaller matching
turn.

00:04:58.024 --> 00:05:05.201 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Tracing the outline and testing a complete revolution gives
evidence, while looking at one corner usually does not.

00:05:05.201 --> 00:05:05.361 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A student says a non-square rectangle has order four because
it has four sides.

00:05:05.361 --> 00:05:12.921 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Test that claim with a quarter turn.

00:05:12.921 --> 00:05:16.881 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Will a long horizontal edge cover a short vertical edge?

00:05:16.881 --> 00:05:21.380 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Decide which matching turns are actually supported by the
whole outline.

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:31.740 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The rectangle does not match after a quarter turn, so four
sides do not prove order four.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:35.820 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The half-turn and full-turn positions do match, giving order
two.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:42.815 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The order is determined by exact covering positions around a
centre, not simply by a count of sides or corners.

00:05:42.815 --> 00:05:44.615 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to our learning goal.

00:05:44.615 --> 00:05:47.695 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Describe turns and test rotational symmetry.

00:05:47.695 --> 00:05:52.735 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one example from today and explain the evidence that
made its answer convincing.

00:05:52.735 --> 00:05:57.375 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which of our three success criteria feels secure, and which
needs another try?

00:05:57.375 --> 00:06:03.902 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Revisit a model, test your explanation with a partner, and
describe one mistake you can now notice and correct.
